.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/esrecurse Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "Esrecurse [![Build Status](https://travis-ci.org/estools/esrecurse.svg?branch=master)](https://travis-ci.org/estools/esrecurse)"
.PP
Esrecurse (\fResrecurse\fP) is \fRECMAScript\fP recursive traversing functionality\&.
.SS "Example Usage"
The following code will output all variables declared at the root of a file\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
esrecurse\&.visit(ast, {
    XXXStatement: function (node) {
        this\&.visit(node\&.left);
        // do something\&.\&.\&.
        this\&.visit(node\&.right);
    }
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
We can use \fRVisitor\fP instance\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
var visitor = new esrecurse\&.Visitor({
    XXXStatement: function (node) {
        this\&.visit(node\&.left);
        // do something\&.\&.\&.
        this\&.visit(node\&.right);
    }
});

visitor\&.visit(ast);
.fi
.PP

.PP
We can inherit \fRVisitor\fP instance easily\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
class Derived extends esrecurse\&.Visitor {
    constructor()
    {
        super(null);
    }

    XXXStatement(node) {
    }
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
function DerivedVisitor() {
    esrecurse\&.Visitor\&.call(/* this for constructor */  this  /* visitor object automatically becomes this\&. */);
}
util\&.inherits(DerivedVisitor, esrecurse\&.Visitor);
DerivedVisitor\&.prototype\&.XXXStatement = function (node) {
    this\&.visit(node\&.left);
    // do something\&.\&.\&.
    this\&.visit(node\&.right);
};
.fi
.PP

.PP
And you can invoke default visiting operation inside custom visit operation\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
function DerivedVisitor() {
    esrecurse\&.Visitor\&.call(/* this for constructor */  this  /* visitor object automatically becomes this\&. */);
}
util\&.inherits(DerivedVisitor, esrecurse\&.Visitor);
DerivedVisitor\&.prototype\&.XXXStatement = function (node) {
    // do something\&.\&.\&.
    this\&.visitChildren(node);
};
.fi
.PP

.PP
The \fRchildVisitorKeys\fP option does customize the behaviour of \fRthis\&.visitChildren(node)\fP\&. We can use user-defined node types\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// This tree contains a user\-defined `TestExpression` node\&.
var tree = {
    type: 'TestExpression',

    // This 'argument' is the property containing the other **node**\&.
    argument: {
        type: 'Literal',
        value: 20
    },

    // This 'extended' is the property not containing the other **node**\&.
    extended: true
};
esrecurse\&.visit(
    ast,
    {
        Literal: function (node) {
            // do something\&.\&.\&.
        }
    },
    {
        // Extending the existing traversing rules\&.
        childVisitorKeys: {
            // TargetNodeName: [ 'keys', 'containing', 'the', 'other', '**node**' ]
            TestExpression: ['argument']
        }
    }
);
.fi
.PP

.PP
We can use the \fRfallback\fP option as well\&. If the \fRfallback\fP option is \fR"iteration"\fP, \fResrecurse\fP would visit all enumerable properties of unknown nodes\&. Please note circular references cause the stack overflow\&. AST might have circular references in additional properties for some purpose (e\&.g\&. \fRnode\&.parent\fP)\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
esrecurse\&.visit(
    ast,
    {
        Literal: function (node) {
            // do something\&.\&.\&.
        }
    },
    {
        fallback: 'iteration'
    }
);
.fi
.PP

.PP
If the \fRfallback\fP option is a function, \fResrecurse\fP calls this function to determine the enumerable properties of unknown nodes\&. Please note circular references cause the stack overflow\&. AST might have circular references in additional properties for some purpose (e\&.g\&. \fRnode\&.parent\fP)\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
esrecurse\&.visit(
    ast,
    {
        Literal: function (node) {
            // do something\&.\&.\&.
        }
    },
    {
        fallback: function (node) {
            return Object\&.keys(node)\&.filter(function(key) {
                return key !== 'argument'
            });
        }
    }
);
.fi
.PP
